  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up loss bowl over two settings with simulated batch noise. No real model is trained and no real data is used.",
    "Loss = (2.4 x^2 + 0.4 z^2) / 2, drawn at 0.4 times the loss. A step is -rate × (slope + noise). Along x, swings grow once the rate is above 2/2.4 ≈ 0.83, so rates near 1 diverge.",
    "The noise is random and centred on zero, with spread sqrt((64 - b) / (63 b)) on each slope component: 1 at batch 1, 0 at 64. Noise values come from a fixed seed, run number and step, so Reset replays the same sequence.",
    "Both arrows are steps drawn at 5 times their real length (one scale for both). Purple is the step this mini-batch gives. Dark is the step the full data gives, opposite the true slope. Step length is the purple step in bowl units.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished). It ends early (diverged) if the loss goes over 4.8 or the ball leaves the drawn bowl. Off-bowl points are drawn at the rim, and the Loss readout keeps the true value.",
    "Each trail holds the start point plus one point per step, so up to 41 points. Play runs 5 steps per second. With noise, a single step can raise the loss; steps lower it only on average."
  ]
}